```java
class Controller {
    private Phase current;
    private long phaseStartMillis;

    void tick(long nowMillis) {
        if (nowMillis - phaseStartMillis >= current.getDurationMillis()) {
            Phase next = phaseTable.next(current);
            transitionTo(next, nowMillis);
        }
    }

    private void transitionTo(Phase next, long nowMillis) {
        if (!conflictMatrix.isSafe(next)) {
            throw new UnsafePhaseException(current, next);
        }
        applyPhase(next);
        current = next;
        phaseStartMillis = nowMillis;
    }
}
```

`tick()` runs on a fixed schedule — every 100ms, say — rather than hardware driving transitions
directly. The timer only ever *asks* the controller to check, and `transitionTo` is the one place a
phase change actually happens, which is where the next slide's safety check plugs in.

```java
class ConflictMatrix {
    // conflictsWith.get(A) = SignalGroups that must never be green while A is green
    private final Map<SignalGroup, Set<SignalGroup>> conflictsWith;

    boolean isSafe(Phase proposed) {
        for (SignalGroup a : proposed.getGreenGroups()) {
            for (SignalGroup b : proposed.getGreenGroups()) {
                if (a != b && conflictsWith.get(a).contains(b)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

`ConflictMatrix` is checked on *every* transition, including ones from the normal timed cycle and
ones from an emergency override — there's no code path that applies a `Phase` without going
through `isSafe()` first. That's what turns "this phase table looks correct" into "this can never
be wrong."
